# JNLisp Language Playground

A little workbook for playing with the features of my literate Lisp-based
Domain Specific Language (DSL) for interacting with my CFD library, but
it can also do lots of cool things!  It is reasonably complete with most
Lisps aside from any quoting/macro features (so maybe it's actually not
that complete).  But, for basic algorithms and maths-y stuff it's pretty
convenient.  Here are some examples:

## Core data types

Some errors that should show up as syntax errors

## Let's define a factorial procedure

j(define (fact n) 
  (if (= n 1)
    1
    (* n (fact (- n 1)))))

## Let's try run it

j(fact 4)

j(fact 10)

## Importing Libraries

You can import other libraries with the special form `import` which takes
the name of the library as a string as the first argument and optionally
a specific prefix as a second.  By default anything defined in the library
is prefixed with "lib-name/".  Libraries can currently only be defined
through Go bindings at the moment which means they must be specified
at compile time.  Eventually I'll add dynamic filepath-based import resolution
but given the main use-case is to run in the browser (where the idea of a
filepath is murky at best) it requires some nuance and isn't very important
for now.  My CFD code gets imported by default without a prefix so you
can use it by default.

j(import "maths")

Let's use this to find the square root of 16

j(maths/sqrt 16)

You can also use maths-defined symbols inside user-defined procedures,
for example.

j(define (circle-area r) 
  (* r r maths/pi))

j(circle-area 10)
